WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 213: The United Kingdom: 1066: The Norman Conquest, Hastings, and the Domesday Book

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.000
[Rebecca] Today we stand on the watershed date of English history, the year 1066.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.480
[Rebecca] In a single autumn of blood and iron, the Anglo-Saxon world was swept away by the Norman conquest.

5
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:27.040
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Harold Godwinson, the Battle of Hastings, and the Doomsday Book.

6
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:38.640
[George] In January 1066, King Edward the Confessor died in his newly consecrated Romanesque abbey at Westminster, leaving no child or designated heir to the English throne.

7
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:51.280
[George] The Royal Council of Anglo-Saxon nobles, the Wittenigamote, immediately elected Edward's brother-in-law, Harold Godwinson, the wealthy and militarily capable Earl of Wessex, as King Harold I.

8
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:58.400
[George] But across the English Channel, Duke William of Normandy flew into a cold, calculating fury.

9
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:12.400
[George] William claimed that years earlier, Edward had promised him the English crown, and that in 1064, when Harold was shipwrecked on the coast of Normandy, Harold had sworn a sacred oath on holy relics to support William's claim.

10
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.280
[George] Denouncing Harold as a perjured usurper.

11
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.760
[George] William secured the formal blessing and papal banner of Pope Alexander I.

12
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:51.160
[George] Isty murant Stella, these men marvel at the star, interpreted as an omen of royal doom.

13
00:01:51.160 --> 00:02:07.160
[George] The Latin motto of Edward's reign emphasized piety, yet his failure to leave an undisputed heir plunged England into an existential succession crisis that invited invasion from three separate directions, setting the stage for the dramatic autumn of 1066.

14
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:17.880
[George] While King Harold Godwinson deployed his army along the southern coast of England through the summer of 1066 watching for Norman sails, a mortal danger struck from the north.

15
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:30.280
[George] Harold Hardrada, the gigantic, battle-hardened king of Norway, celebrated as the last Viking invaded Yorkshire with 300 longships, joined by King Harold's traitorous brother, Tostig.

16
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.600
[George] The Vikings rooted local northern forces at Fulford and captured York.

17
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:50.920
[George] In an astonishing military feat, King Harold marched his elite royal housecarls and Saxon levies 185 miles north in just four days, catching the Vikings completely by surprise on September 25, 1066, at Stamford Bridge.

18
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:58.120
[George] The Vikings, having left their heavy chainmill armour aboard their ships in the unseasonable heat, were utterly crushed.

19
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.120
[George] Both Harold Hardrata and Tostig were killed in the bloody melee.

20
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:10.920
[George] Out of three hundred invading Viking ships, only twenty four were needed to carry the survivors home, ending the Viking Age in Britain forever.

21
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:25.720
[George] King Harold's lightning northern march of nearly two hundred miles in four days remains one of the greatest military marches in medieval history, catching Harold Hardrada's Vikings without their armor, resting beside the river Derwent in false security.

22
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:38.040
[George] The Battle of Stamford Bridge saw the death of Harold Hardrada, pierced in the neck by an arrow, concluding three centuries of terrifying Viking coastal raids that had shaped British history since Lindisfarne.

23
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:49.040
[George] Just three days after his triumphant victory at Stamford Bridge, while Harold was celebrating in York, an exhausted horseman galloped into the banqueting hall with catastrophic news.

24
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.520
[George] The wind across the Channel had changed.

25
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:57.520
[George] And Duke William of Normandy had landed on the southern coast at Pevensey Bay on September 28.

26
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:10.000
[George] William had erected a prefabricated wooden Mot and Bailey Castle at Hastings, ravaging the surrounding countryside of Sussex, Harold's ancestral heartland, to bait the Saxon king into immediate battle.

27
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.240
[George] Harold made a fateful decision.

28
00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:26.320
[George] Rather than resting his battered army and allowing Southern levies to mobilize, Harold ordered another grueling forced march south, covering nearly 200 miles in seven days, arriving in London and pushing directly toward the coast.

29
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:35.440
[George] Harold's brothers, Geoth and Leofwine, begged him to remain in London and adopt a scorched earth policy, starving William out in the coming winter.

30
00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:43.680
[George] Driven by personal honor and outrage at the devastation of his people, Harold pressed forward to confront the Norman invader on the field.

31
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:59.120
[George] William of Normandy's invasion fleet carried over two thousand warhorses across the channel, utilizing specially constructed flat-bottom transports with stern ramps, an unprecedented amphibious undertaking that required months of meticulous logistics.

32
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.440
[George] The Norman fleet waited for weeks at St.

33
00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.800
[George] Valerie sur Somme, praying before the relics of St.

34
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:11.440
[George] Valerie for favourable southerly winds, which Duke William interpreted as divine sanction for his invasion.

35
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:21.840
[George] At dawn on Saturday, October 14, 1066, the two armies drew up for battle on the slope of Senlac Hill, seven miles north of Hastings.

36
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] King Harold deployed 7,000 Anglo-Saxon infantry atop the ridgeline in a dense, unbroken shield wall spanning 600 yards.

37
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:43.880
[George] Their interlocking wooden and leather shields locked together like an iron wall, with his royal housecarls in front wielding massive, two-handed Danish battle axes capable of cleaving an armoured knight and horse in a single blow.

38
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:56.040
[George] At nine in the morning, William advanced his army in three combined arms lines, archers in front, heavy armoured infantry in the center, and noble Norman cavalry on warhorses in the rear.

39
00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:05.880
[George] The Norman archers loosed volleys of arrows, but firing uphill, their shafts bounced harmlessly off the overlapping Saxon shields or flew over their heads.

40
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:17.800
[George] When the Norman infantry and cavalry charged up the steep, muddy slope, the Saxon shield wall held immovable, swinging battle axes that cut down horses and knights with terrifying butchery.

41
00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:33.080
[George] The Anglo-Saxon shield wall atop Senlac Hill was so dense that contemporary chroniclers noted the dead could not fall to the ground, remaining wedged upright between their living comrades as the heavy two-handed Danish battle axes cleaved Norman chainmill.

42
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:45.640
[George] Harold's elite housecarls formed the veteran corps of the Saxon army, sworn by sacred oaths of personal loyalty to die beside their king, wielding their deadly axes with rhythmically brutal efficiency.

43
00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:52.760
[George] By early afternoon, after hours of brutal, indecisive slaughter, panic erupted on the Norman left flank.

44
00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:56.360
[George] A rumor swept the lines that Duke William had been killed.

45
00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.800
[George] Breton and Norman knights broke and fled down the slope.

46
00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:09.400
[George] Defying Harold's strict orders to maintain the shield wall, hundreds of undisciplined Saxon militia broke ranks and pursued the fleeing Normans down the hill.

47
00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:20.640
[George] William seized the moment, pushing back his nasal helmet to reveal his face, he shouted to his retreating knights Look at me, I live, and with God's help I shall conquer.

48
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:26.800
[George] Turning his heavy cavalry, William surrounded and annihilated the isolated Saxons at the foot of the hill.

49
00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:36.800
[George] Recognizing the tactic, William ordered two more deliberate feigned retreats, luring more Saxons out of their defensive formation and thinning the shield wall.

50
00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:44.960
[George] Late in the afternoon, William ordered his archers to shoot high into the air so their arrows plunged straight down behind the Saxon shields.

51
00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:49.040
[George] In that deadly hail, an arrow struck King Harold in the eye.

52
00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:59.760
[George] As Harold ripped the arrow from his skull in agony, four Norman knights broke through the crumbling line and hacked the Saxon king to pieces beneath his golden dragon of Wessex standard.

53
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:06.480
[George] The Norman feigned retreat was an ancient tactical maneuver that Duke William had practiced in continental campaigns.

54
00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:16.160
[George] By simulating panic, William induced the eager, inexperienced Saxon Feat Levies to break their defensive line, transforming a stalemate into a rout.

55
00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.720
[George] The feigned retreat of the Norman left wing was executed with deadly precision.

56
00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:29.040
[George] As the undisciplined Saxon militia broke ranks, Norman cavalry encircled them on the marshy ground, cutting them down without mercy.

57
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.160
[George] With King Harold and his brothers dead, Anglo Saxon resistance disintegrated.

58
00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:46.160
[George] William marched through Kent, burning towns and circling London to cut off supplies until the remaining Saxon nobles, including Archbishop Stigend and Edgar the Etheling, surrendered at Berkhamstead.

59
00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:55.520
[George] On Christmas Day 1066, inside Edward the Confessor's new abbey at Westminster, Duke William was crowned King William I of England.

60
00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.400
[George] The coronation was marked by dark irony.

61
00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:07.880
[George] When the Norman and Saxon bishops asked the congregation in French and English if they accepted William as king, the assembly shouted their assent with loud cheers.

62
00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:18.760
[George] Outside the abbey, Norman cavalry guards, mistaking the shouts of joy for a treacherous Saxon riot, immediately torched the surrounding houses and began looting the neighborhood.

63
00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:30.600
[George] Inside, as smoke filled the abbey and guests fled in terror, a trembling William took the royal oath before the altar amidst the crackle of burning timber, beginning his reign in smoke and flame.

64
00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:46.840
[George] William's coronation in Westminster Abbey began an era of bilingual English governance, where the royal court, aristocracy, and legal courts operated in Anglo Norman French, while the common populace spoke Old English, gradually fusing into Middle English.

65
00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:58.760
[George] William's coronation marked the permanent introduction of Norman feudal architecture, beginning the construction of the White Tower of the Tower of London to dominate and intimidate the rebellious citizens of London.

66
00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:06.840
[George] To secure his conquest against repeated Saxon rebellions, William instituted the most profound transformation in English history.

67
00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:26.760
[George] In 1069, facing a major northern uprising backed by Danish fleets, William launched the brutal harrying of the north, systematically burning crops, salting fields, slaughtering livestock, and levelling villages across Yorkshire, creating an artificial famine that starved one hundred thousand people.

68
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:37.560
[George] To hold the kingdom, William covered England with hundreds of stone castles, replacing the entire Anglo Saxon nobility with Norman barons and introducing feudal land tenure.

69
00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:57.520
[George] In 1085, William commissioned the Doomsday Book, an exhaustive parish by parish survey of every acre of land, ox, plough, mill, and pig in England, conducted with such terrifying thoroughness that contemporary Saxons remarked not one ox escaped notice, resembling the final day of judgment.

70
00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:12.080
[George] The Norman Conquest fused Germanic Anglo-Saxon and French culture, injecting 10,000 French words into the English vocabulary, forging the dynamic language, legal tradition, and constitutional identity of modern Britain.

71
00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:28.640
[George] The Doomsday Book, bound in two thick volumes and housed in the Royal Treasury at Winchester, was compiled in just seven months by traveling royal commissioners who took sworn testimony from local juries, establishing an administrative baseline of royal property taxation.

72
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:42.880
[George] The Doomsday Survey classified England into shires, hundreds, and manors, recording the names of every tenant in chief, creating an unshakable legal record of royal ownership that transformed English common law.

73
00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:51.040
[Rebecca] The Norman Conquest united the British Isles under a centralized crown, laying the foundation for England's rise as a global power.

74
00:11:51.040 --> 00:12:00.320
[Rebecca] Across the English Channel, the ancient kingdom of France was forged in an equally fiery crucible of faith, war, and royal majesty.

75
00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.040
[Rebecca] Next week, we enter the storied realm of France with episode 214.

